# Lint strategy: warn burn-down + diff ratchet

## The problem

Turning strict lint rules to `error` on a mature codebase often yields thousands of violations. A big-bang fix stalls delivery. Ignoring rules lets debt compound.

## The pattern (two speeds)

| Speed | Scope | Severity | Purpose |
| -------------- | ------------------ | ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| **Global** | Whole codebase | `warn` (or advisory) | Visibility + burn-down tracking |
| **Diff-aware** | Changed files only | `error` on regression | New work cannot make things worse |
| **Pre-commit** | Staged hunks | Stricter than global | Catch issues before they enter history |
| **Agents** | New/generated code | Treat `warn` as `error` | AI does not inherit legacy excuses |

## Shared preset modules

Split lint policy into **one concern per config module** (not one giant config):

- Complexity & control flow (`max-depth`, `complexity`, `max-lines`)
- Magic numbers & literals
- Environment / config access boundaries
- Import restrictions & alias bans
- Naming (abbreviations, conventions)
- Framework-specific rules — isolated per consumer package

Each module documents: what it enforces, why severity is `warn` vs `error`, and which human doc it mirrors.

## Recommended thresholds (starting point — tune per team)

| Rule family | Suggested ceiling | Intent |
| --------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Nesting depth | 1 level per function | Guard clauses over nested `if` |
| Cyclomatic complexity | 10 | Split branchy functions |
| Lines per function | 50 (target 20–30) | Single responsibility |
| Lines per file | 300 | Split by responsibility |
| Cognitive complexity | 15 | Readability beyond branch count |

**Encoded in presets** ([`../references/lint/`](../references/lint/README.md)):

| Preset | Nesting | Cyclomatic | Fn lines | File lines | Cognitive |
| ------ | ------- | ---------- | -------- | ---------- | --------- |
| Oxlint (default) | `max-depth: 1` | `complexity: 10` | `max-lines-per-function: 50` | `max-lines: 300` | — (review / Biome) |
| ESLint | `max-depth: 1` | `complexity: 10` | `max-lines-per-function: 50` | `max-lines: 300` | — |
| Biome | — | — | — | — | `noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity: 15` |
| Ruff | `max-nested-blocks: 1` | `max-complexity: 10` | `max-statements: 50` | — | — |

Tests may relax fn-line / statements ceilings (see preset overrides). Change numbers here **and** in the matching preset — keep the table authoritative.

## The diff-aware ratchet (two layers)

Compare each touched file to its state at the **merge-base** (where the branch diverged from the target). Not "is the file clean?" but "did this PR worsen it?"

### Layer 1 — no-regression

| Condition | Blocks merge? |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Violation on a line **added** by this PR | **Yes** |
| Count of "universal" violations **increased** (complexity, file size, etc.) | **Yes** — only the **surplus** |
| Pre-existing violations unchanged | No — optional warning for visibility |

**Guarantee:** a passing PR never raises violation counts on touched files.

### Layer 2 — capped boy-scout descent

When a touched file already has **auto-fixable** debt:

- Retire at least **K** items (team constant, often 3)
- **Cap at K** — a 40-item file owes 3, not 40
- Exempt rules that imply large refactors (e.g. splitting a 900-line file)
- Files with fewer than K items must reach zero
- Optional **waiver label** skips Layer 2; Layer 1 still applies

### Merge hygiene (ratchet accuracy)

| Do | Don't |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Merge the target branch with a normal, conflict-respecting merge | Cross-merge feature branches into each other |
| Minimize blast radius in legacy files | Re-format entire legacy files for a one-line fix |
| Fix the surplus the gate reports | Disable rules file-wide to get green |
| Use auto-fix for mechanical boy-scout items | Rewrite whole blocks during conflict resolution |

## Scoped lint & format (critical during burn-down)

**Never** run whole-repo `lint --fix` / `format --write` while a warn backlog exists — it creates a giant unrelated diff.

```bash
# Pattern: lint only what you changed, in the owning package/workspace
cd <package-root>
<linter> --fix path/to/changed-file.ts
<formatter> --write path/to/changed-file.ts
```

Order: **linter --fix** → **formatter** → **verify zero errors on touched files**.

Starter configs + hook wiring: [`../references/lint/`](../references/lint/README.md) (installed by [`setup-skills`](../skills/setup-skills/SKILL.md)).

## Configuration externalization

Two rules, often enforced by a diff-aware "config literal" scanner:

1. **Explaining variables** — bare numbers/strings in logic become named constants (whitelist: `0`, `1`, `2`, indexes, defaults).
2. **Externalize policy-varying values** — timeouts, retention, quotas, limits live in env/config with sensible defaults. Name env vars with the **unit the runtime consumes** (`_MS`, `_SECONDS`, `_DAYS`).

